ormed to  the probability scale and what you get  for that is that your standard error  bands for the probabilities all lie  within zero and one so this guy never  gets below zero it's wedged in at zero  but never gets below zero and that's  kind of what you'd like okay and now we  add some points to show where the data  occur  um and they'll appear there they are and  and I've actually what I've done here is  I first of all use Jitter of age because  there were lots of ties in age at each  of the integer values of age to give an  indication of how many how much data  occurred at each age I used the Jitter  function which randomly adds to each  element of the vector age a little bit  of uniform random noise so you can get  an idea of the density so there are  these zeros they're the ones  to have them fit on the page since I'd  limited the scale to up to 0.1 which  means that we can see here only four  percent at most of the population in any  given age category earned above 250k  we had to limit the range we had to  shrink down the ones to point to make  them point one so we can include them  and just see them in the plot okay so  this is similar to a plot we have in the  in the book and it shows the fitted  probability  okay  so we've learned how to use polynomials  we've learned how to compare different  polynomials and we've learned how to  plot the fitted functions with the  standard error bands and we've also  learned how to use them with glms as  well as linear models  so in the next segment we're going to  look at different functions we're going  to look at splines which are more  General functions  usually better behaved than polynomials  and we're going to see how to fit  splines both with single variables and  with multiple variables using the gam  package
 

